.TH TOP 1 "Substrate" "Substrate General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
top \- display and update sorted information about processes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B top
.RB [ \-bcHs ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR secs ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR count ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR pid [,\fIpid\fR...]]
.RB [ \-u
.IR user ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B top
provides a dynamic real-time view of the running system: a summary
header followed by a continuously updated, sorted list of processes.
It is patterned on
.Xr procps 1
.BR top ,
running on the Substrate process and VM introspection system calls.
.PP
By default
.B top
runs interactively, redrawing every 3 seconds, and reads single-key
commands from the terminal.  With
.B \-b
it instead prints plain-text frames to standard output, one per
iteration, suitable for logging.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " secs"
Set the refresh delay in seconds (fractional accepted).  Default 3.0.
.TP
.BI \-n " count"
Exit after producing
.I count
frames.  Default is to run until told to quit.
.TP
.BI \-p " pid[,pid...]"
Monitor only the listed process IDs (up to 20).
.TP
.BI \-u " user"
Show only processes owned by
.IR user
(a name or numeric UID).
.TP
.B \-b
Batch mode: no terminal control, no input handling.
.TP
.B \-s
Secure mode: disable the
.BR k ,
.B r
and configuration-changing commands.
.TP
.B \-H
Start in threads view.
.TP
.B \-c
Show the full command line in the COMMAND column.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version and exit.
.SH "SUMMARY HEADER"
The five header lines report, in order: the current time, uptime,
logged-in user count and load averages; task counts by state; the CPU
time split
.RB ( us
user,
.B sy
system,
.B id
idle \(em the
.BR ni ,
.BR wa ,
.BR hi ,
.B si
and
.B st
categories are not yet tracked by the kernel and read 0); physical
memory; and swap.
.SH "PROCESS COLUMNS"
.TP
.B PID
Process ID.
.TP
.B USER
Effective user name (or numeric UID if unresolved).
.TP
.B PR
Scheduling priority (normal tasks show NI + 20).
.TP
.B NI
Nice value, \-20 (highest) to 19 (lowest).
.TP
.B VIRT
Virtual memory size.
.TP
.B RES
Resident set size.
.TP
.B SHR
Shared memory (currently always 0; not yet tracked).
.TP
.B S
Process state: R running, S sleeping, T stopped, Z zombie, I idle.
.TP
.B %CPU
CPU share since the previous refresh, computed from the user+system
jiffy delta over the wall-clock delta.
.TP
.B %MEM
Resident set as a percentage of total physical memory.
.TP
.B TIME+
Total CPU time consumed, minutes:seconds.hundredths.
.TP
.B COMMAND
Command name, or full command line under
.B \-c
or the
.B c
command.
.SH "INTERACTIVE COMMANDS"
.TP
.B q
Quit; the terminal is always restored on the way out.
.TP
.BR Space ", " Enter
Refresh immediately.
.TP
.BR P ", " M ", " T ", " N
Sort by %CPU, %MEM, TIME+ or PID respectively.
.TP
.B R
Reverse the sort order (default is descending).
.TP
.B i
Toggle hiding of idle processes (%CPU < 0.05).
.TP
.B c
Toggle the COMMAND column between name and full command line.
.TP
.B u
Filter to a single user; empty input clears the filter.
.TP
.BR d ", " s
Change the refresh delay.
.TP
.B k
Kill a process: prompts for a PID, then a signal (default 15).
.TP
.B r
Renice a process: prompts for a PID, then a nice value.
.TP
.BR h ", " ?
Show the interactive help screen.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.toprc
Optional configuration read at startup;
.B key = value
lines with
.B #
comments.  Recognized keys:
.BR delay ,
.BR sort ,
.BR idle ,
.BR cmdline .
.SH EXAMPLES
Capture three snapshots a half-second apart into a log:
.PP
.RS
.nf
top \-b \-n 3 \-d 0.5 > /var/log/top.out
.fi
.RE
.PP
Watch a single service by PID:
.PP
.RS
.nf
top \-p 1423
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Returns 0 on a clean exit, 1 on a fatal error (such as failure to
allocate working buffers).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.Xr ps 1 ,
.Xr kill 1 ,
.Xr renice 8
.SH NOTES
The
.BR ni / wa / hi / si / st
CPU categories, the SHR column, and threads view are limited by what
the current kernel exposes; they are placeholders pending kernel
support.
